// main.dog: this chat's bot. It runs on every message that isn't a /command,
// and whatever it outputs is the reply. No output means no reply.
//
// The message arrives in globals: $message is the text, $sender who sent it,
// and $reply / $reply_from the message it answered, if any.
//
// dog4 in one breath: statements end with `;` (newlines don't), `$x` reads a
// variable and `x = …` sets one, `{ cmd args }` runs a command and gives its
// value, and `( … )` holds operators: ($n + 1), ($a == $b).
// Try things in the playground: https://henriquecolini.github.io/dog4/

use read, write from mod bot;

words = { split $message };
command = { lower ($words[0] ?? "") };

// An `if` on its own throws its value away, so the reply is wrapped in
// `put ( … )`. When no branch matches, the `if` is null and nothing is sent.
put (if ($command == ping) {
    put pong
} else if ($command == hi || $command == hello) {
    put "Hi $sender! Edit main.dog to teach me something new."
} else if ($command == count) {
    // Files outlive the message. `read` raises when the file is missing, and
    // `try … catch` turns that into a 0.
    n = try { read count.txt } catch e 0;
    n = (num($n) + 1);
    write count.txt $n;
    put "I've counted to $n."
})
